      "text": "Ada Tours is not responsible for hotel breakfasts missed because an early flight or excursion requires early check-out."
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_title:5cd7a3d146d31311",
      "field": "itinerary.title",
      "sourceTextSha256": "0737848eb207f2baac3de86a9510a7f7e47ba10d94d541a89a4cf3a36e78c8b4",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-20261110-work-8cfa443091a8",
        "model": "GPT-6.1"
      },
      "text": "Hito Cajones, Laguna Verde, Sol de Mañana and Laguna Colorada"
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:5a4836cca1208c95",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "0c0d1716dcf95af6c5e18d76d475002556feca0f6df0abc42d786844436c59b3",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-20261110-work-8cfa443091a8",
        "model": "GPT-6.1"
      },
      "text": "The tour starts at 10:00 a.m. Bolivian time at Hito Cajones, on the border between Chile and Bolivia. Your driver meets the group with a sign showing your names.\n\nThe first leg crosses high-altitude landscapes to Laguna Verde, with views of Licancabur volcano. The route continues through the Salvador Dalí Valley, known for its large rock formations, to the Polques Hot Springs and the Sol de Mañana geysers.\n\nThe geysers are active, constantly releasing steam and hot mud. From there, continue to Laguna Colorada, a red lake at about 4,400 m above sea level. Rare James’s flamingos visit the lake to feed on plankton.\n\nSpend the night at an expedition-style hotel in the Eduardo Avaroa National Reserve.\n\nReserve admission costs 150 bolivianos and is paid separately."
    },
